## 🛰️ Orientation

A letter was written by somebody to somebody.

That sounds almost insultingly obvious. It is also increasingly important.

Historical correspondence can now be digitised, transcribed, indexed, searched, analysed and potentially incorporated into very large computational datasets. Some of those uses can enormously increase human access to the past. A fragile letter held in one archive can be scanned so that thousands of people can read it without damaging the original. Handwriting can be transcribed. Collections can become searchable. Researchers can connect people and events that previously required years of archival travel to reconstruct.

None of that means every technically possible use of a letter is therefore equivalent.

There is a meaningful difference between preserving correspondence so that humans can encounter the people who left it behind and acquiring correspondence primarily because the language contained within it has extractive computational value.

There is a still greater difference if digitisation involves disbinding, destruction, disposal or the disappearance of the physical artefact itself.

This node begins from an unapologetically human proposition:

**As a human being, that feels wrong. It feels like grave robbing.**

That reaction should not automatically be dismissed because it cannot be reduced to a property right, copyright term or machine-learning benchmark.

Sometimes moral intuitions are signals that the available technical categories are failing to describe what humans actually believe is being protected.

