## 🖋️ A Letter Is Not Merely Text

A letter has textual content.

It is not therefore merely text.

It may also have:

- handwriting;
- corrections;
- crossings-out;
- marginalia;
- paper and stationery;
- folds;
- envelopes;
- postmarks;
- stains and physical wear;
- forms of address;
- jokes understood only between particular people;
- changes in handwriting caused by age or circumstance;
- references whose meaning depends upon a relationship;
- evidence about when, where and how it travelled;
- a provenance connecting one person to another.

Even the digressions matter.

A grandfather who begins a family letter with Napoleon's return and somehow reaches much of the early twentieth century within two sheets of letter paper has left more than a collection of historically themed sentences.

He has left evidence of **how his mind moved**.

What he assumed his reader knew. What one event reminded him of. Which connections seemed sufficiently obvious that they needed no explanation. What deserved three paragraphs before he finally got to whatever he ostensibly sat down to write about.

Extract the words and some information survives.

Preserve the letter and something more survives:

**a human act.**

---

## 📮 The Preposition Matters

A published book and a private letter begin with different communicative architectures.

A book is deliberately placed into circulation.

A letter begins:

**sender → intended recipient**

Someone wrote it **to** someone.

That preposition is doing considerable ethical work.

It does not follow that correspondence must remain private forever. Families donate papers. Estates make decisions. Archives preserve collections. Historians publish correspondence. Researchers encounter material its author probably never imagined strangers reading.

Historical scholarship would be immeasurably poorer if nobody could ever encounter private correspondence after the death of its participants.

But neither does death magically transform:

> I wrote this to Edith.

into:

> I intended these words to become unrestricted raw material for every technological system humanity might subsequently invent.

Those are different propositions.

Recognising that difference does not require pretending that the dead possess exactly the same legal privacy rights as the living.

It requires recognising **context**.

---

## 🪦 It Feels Like Grave Robbing

There are circumstances in which ordinary human moral language identifies something that technical language struggles to hold.

This is one of them.

If historically significant correspondence were purchased principally because its contents had computational value, extracted for that purpose, and particularly if the physical material were subsequently destroyed or discarded, it would feel uncomfortably like grave robbing.

Letters are not human remains.

The analogy is not literal.

The relevant similarity is that **the inability of the dead to defend something does not make taking it morally neutral**.

Human societies have repeatedly recognised obligations surrounding things associated intimately with people who can no longer express preferences themselves.

Those obligations are not reducible to utility.

Nor are they necessarily absolute.

They are obligations of custody, context, dignity and stewardship.

The question is therefore not simply:

> Can this material be useful?

It is also:

> What kind of relationship with the dead does this use represent?

---

## 🩻 Anatomists Needed Bodies

Medicine provides an uncomfortable historical analogy.

Human anatomy genuinely required access to cadavers. Medical education genuinely benefited from dissection. Knowledge obtained through anatomical study genuinely saved lives.

Those facts did not, by themselves, determine how human bodies could legitimately be acquired.

Where legitimate supply was restricted and demand remained high, illicit acquisition emerged around anatomical medicine. Body snatching and associated practices became part of a much larger cultural history concerning medicine, death, bodily custody and public trust.

The lesson is not:

**AI training is equivalent to anatomical dissection.**

Nor should later medical scandals or contemporary attitudes towards donation be attributed to one simple historical cause.

The useful lesson is narrower:

> **A legitimate institutional objective does not legitimise every means of obtaining the material required to pursue it.**

Or more simply:

**Anatomists needed bodies.**

Yes.

And the manner in which bodies were obtained still mattered.

The same reasoning applies to data-intensive technologies.

A model may benefit from more data.

A researcher may genuinely want rare linguistic material.

A particular corpus may improve performance.

None of those observations independently answers the question:

**How may the material legitimately be obtained and used?**

Need is not consent.

Scientific usefulness is not consent.

Commercial usefulness is not consent.

Scarcity is not consent.

And difficulty obtaining sufficient material through legitimate channels is not consent.

