## ☔️ Institutional Memory Outlives The Incident

There is another reason to care about the manner of acquisition.

Institutions do not interact only with the material immediately in front of them.

They interact with **future trust**.

When people learn that material entrusted for one purpose may quietly be used for another, the consequence can extend beyond the particular use.

People alter their behaviour.

Families may withhold collections.

Owners may refuse digitisation.

Authors may destroy correspondence.

Communities may distrust archives.

Potential donors may decide that the only reliable means of controlling future use is never surrendering custody in the first place.

The historical relationship between medicine, bodily custody and donation illustrates the broader governance problem: professional claims of necessity can leave cultural residues when ordinary expectations about custody are repeatedly overridden.

That matters enormously for AI.

If future researchers want people voluntarily to contribute diaries, correspondence, oral histories, photographs, manuscripts and other intimate cultural records for computational scholarship, then respecting the boundaries around today's material is not an obstacle to that future.

**It is one of its prerequisites.**

