## 🗝️ Contextual Privacy

A person can speak somewhere another human being can hear them without thereby agreeing that the conversation may be:

- permanently recorded;
- aggregated with millions of others;
- computationally analysed;
- commercially licensed;
- incorporated into a model;
- or retained indefinitely.

The same contextual distinction exists in correspondence.

Private communication does not become conceptually public merely because somebody eventually gains physical possession of the medium carrying it.

Letters make the problem still more complicated because correspondence rarely contains information exclusively about its author.

A letter may contain:

- another person's medical information;
- descriptions of children;
- sexuality;
- family secrets;
- financial difficulties;
- allegations;
- gossip;
- political beliefs;
- descriptions of relationships;
- addresses and movements;
- information about people who may still be alive.

A single historical letter can therefore contain overlapping interests belonging to its writer, recipient, subjects, descendants, owner, archive and wider historical community.

Property law can resolve who owns the sheet of paper.

That does not necessarily resolve every ethical question concerning what may be done with the human information written upon it.

---

## 📚 Preservation Is Not Extraction

Digitisation itself is not the enemy.

Digitisation can be preservation.

A manuscript too fragile for routine handling can be made globally accessible.

A scholar thousands of miles away can read correspondence previously available only to people capable of travelling to one archive.

Transcription can make difficult handwriting accessible.

Search can reveal relationships across enormous collections.

Machine learning itself may eventually become extraordinarily useful for reconstructing, indexing, translating and understanding historical archives.

The relevant distinction is therefore not:

**analogue good / digital bad**

or:

**human good / machine bad.**

It is between different relationships with cultural material.

### Preservation

> We made a digital representation so that the artefact survives and more humans can encounter it.

### Scholarship

> We studied this material because understanding these people, events or cultures matters.

### Extraction

> We acquired this object because information contained within it has downstream computational value.

Those categories can overlap.

But they should not be deliberately collapsed.

