## 💰 When The Extractor Can Outbid The Keeper

There is also an economic question.

Historical letters are already bought and sold.

Collectors may have complicated motives, and private ownership can itself remove important material from public access. Collecting should not automatically be romanticised.

But one extremely ordinary human motive for collecting historical objects is:

> Someone made this. Someone touched this. Someone kept this. I would like to look after it.

That creates a particular economic relationship with the artefact.

**The collector generally requires the object to continue existing.**

An extractor may value something different.

If a letter is worth £200 as a collectible artefact but substantially more as unusually scarce linguistic or historical training material, a new bidder enters the market whose economic interest lies principally in what can be derived from the object.

That does not mean such a bidder will destroy it.

It does mean that **preservation is no longer necessarily inherent in the economic purpose of acquisition**.

That incentive deserves attention before it becomes consequential.

Governance should not wait until historically valuable material has demonstrably disappeared before asking whether technological demand could distort markets for cultural artefacts.

