## 🔬 The Research Question

This distinction is important because the ethical argument should not be used to smuggle factual claims into the record.

There are separate empirical questions requiring investigation:

1. Have frontier AI developers, contractors, data suppliers or associated organisations purchased scarce physical books, manuscripts or correspondence for digitisation or model-development purposes?

2. Have any relevant acquisition or digitisation pipelines involved disbinding, destruction, disposal, loss or reduced preservation of the physical artefacts?

3. Are AI-related buyers materially affecting markets for scarce books, correspondence or other historical textual artefacts?

4. What contractual, archival or preservation requirements apply when cultural material is acquired for computational use?

5. Can provenance survive ingestion sufficiently well for later researchers to reconstruct where material originated?

These questions should be evidenced rather than presumed.

But the governance problem exists even before the worst-case scenario is demonstrated.

**Do not build incentives under which destruction becomes rational.**

---

## 🌱 Consent Can Be Generous

None of this requires treating people as inherently hostile to research or technology.

People routinely consent to remarkable uses of their bodies, records and possessions when they understand the arrangement and trust the institution receiving them.

Someone can perfectly reasonably decide:

> After I die, preserve my correspondence and make it available for historical and computational research.

Someone else might donate their complete literary archive.

Someone might deliberately release decades of writing as an open corpus.

Someone might explicitly want future models trained upon everything they ever wrote.

Excellent.

The important thing is that **generosity exists because a person was permitted to be generous**.

Taking first and explaining afterwards destroys the very social conditions under which people willingly give.

