## 🧿 Human-To-Human Transmission

There remains something technologically mundane and culturally profound about an old letter changing hands.

Someone writes it.

Someone receives it.

Someone keeps it.

A child inherits it.

A bookseller finds it.

A collector recognises it.

Someone researches the names.

An archive eventually acquires it.

A historian reads it.

A student encounters it a century later.

None of those people needed to know one another.

The object nevertheless moved through a chain of human custody.

That is cultural transmission in an extraordinarily literal form.

Its inefficiency is not necessarily a defect.

Its physicality is not necessarily obsolete.

Its inability to be instantaneously scaled is not evidence that something has gone wrong.

Sometimes civilisation consists of somebody saying:

**I will look after this until somebody else can.**

---

## 🐉 Sauron's Machinery And The Dead Hobbitses

There is an absurdity created when technological systems develop faster than the cultural concepts required to explain them.

We cannot explain Sauron's machinery to the dead hobbitses.

We cannot return to someone writing an intimate letter in 1912 and provide a comprehensible explanation that their patterns of syntax, vocabulary, personality and association might eventually have economic value as training material for a planetary-scale statistical system.

That does not automatically prohibit every future use they could not have anticipated.

Human culture could barely function under such a rule.

But inability to obtain retroactive consent should produce **care**, not opportunism.

Where people cannot possibly participate in the decision, the obligations upon the custodian become greater, not smaller.

---

## ⚖️ Guard The Letters

The argument is therefore not that AI is bad.

It is not that historical documents should never be digitised.

It is not that private collections are inherently virtuous.

It is not that archives must become mausoleums.

It is not that every letter must remain unread except by its intended recipient.

And it is not that computational research has no legitimate place in historical scholarship.

The proposition is simpler.

**AI is not worth everything.**

No model, benchmark, company or technological programme possesses an automatic claim superior to the continued existence of the cultural ecosystem from which it extracts value.

Particularly not because enormously wealthy institutions have decided that acquiring more material is technologically useful.

The archive is allowed to remain inconvenient.

The collector is allowed to love the object.

The family is allowed to care what happens to it.

The scholar is allowed to distinguish reading from extraction.

The dead are allowed to be treated as something other than an unclaimed dataset.

And the letter is allowed to remain what it always was:

**something one human being gave to another.**

Guard the letters because the dead entrusted us with a past.

Guard the letters because somebody may still want to hold them.

Guard the letters because preservation and extraction are not synonyms.

And guard the letters because someday we will want living people to trust us with theirs.

